## What does the audit price cover and when does it cost more?

The £495 fixed fee covers the investigation, the written report, the spreadsheet and the 60-minute walkthrough for a standard site of up to around 500 pages. It does not include development work, content writing, link building or an ongoing monthly programme.

Implementation support is scoped separately after the audit, with the work, responsibilities and price agreed first. You can also implement the actions with your own team.

The audit costs more outside the standard scope: more than around 500 pages, multiple international versions, complex JavaScript rendering or a recent migration. I confirm this in the initial scope, before any paid work starts. Complex enterprise architectures may also need a scoped discussion first.

The £495 is credited against your first month when you start a retainer within 60 days of delivery. There is no minimum contract.

## Who is the audit for?

The audit suits a site with a defined technical question or unexplained organic-search problem. Common triggers include:

- important pages missing from Google
- a traffic drop after a migration or platform change
- duplicate or parameter URLs consuming crawl attention
- templates with inconsistent canonical or indexation rules
- slow or unstable page experiences
- a development backlog that needs evidence-based priorities
- an agency contract coming up for renewal

A brand new site with fewer than 10 pages needs a smaller scope. Weak product-market demand, or a need for someone to implement every change, calls for a different service. Those needs require a different scope.

## Can you review my current SEO agency's work independently before I renew?

Yes. Send the site URL, the renewal date and any reports your agency has given you. The audit looks at the site as it stands today: technical health, content and AI-search visibility. You get a ranked list of findings with evidence, so you can compare it with what the agency reports and what has actually changed.

The audit does not include managing the agency or negotiating the contract. If the findings show work that needs doing, the walkthrough covers what needs a developer, what you can handle yourself and what ongoing support would cover.

## Does the audit cover mobile SEO?

Yes. Google crawls and ranks the mobile version of a site first. The audit checks that version. That includes responsive behaviour, mobile usability, whether important content is available on mobile and Core Web Vitals field data across device types. A separate mobile-only audit is not needed for a standard site.

## What happens after the audit?

Start with the highest-impact validated actions. Assign each action to an owner and record the acceptance check before implementation begins.

Measure crawling and indexation separately from rankings and enquiries. A corrected technical issue can remove a constraint without creating demand or making a page more relevant than its competitors.

Implementation remains a separate decision. You can use your existing developer or request a separate implementation scope after reviewing the findings.

## How do you request the audit?

Send the website URL and the main problem you want investigated. Mention any recent migration, redesign, traffic drop, agency renewal date or platform constraint that could affect the scope.

The fixed £495 fee applies to standard sites of up to around 500 pages. Delivery is within 5 working days after access and scope are confirmed. The 60-minute walkthrough is included.

If the audit shows you need ongoing technical work, see my [technical SEO services](/services/technical-seo-services/).

